The application must validate the amount and lock the idempotent request first. This fragment shows the account operation; do not run the successful branch after a zero-row update.

```sql
START TRANSACTION;
SELECT id, balance, status FROM accounts WHERE id = 101 FOR UPDATE;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 5000
WHERE id = 101 AND status = 'ACTIVE' AND balance >= 5000;
SELECT ROW_COUNT() AS debited;
-- Application: if debited != 1, ROLLBACK and stop.
-- Only the debited = 1 branch continues:
INSERT INTO account_transactions
(account_id, type, amount, status, reference_no, created_at)
VALUES (101, 'WITHDRAWAL', 5000, 'SUCCESS', 'WD-10001', NOW());
COMMIT;
```

If ledger insertion fails, roll back the debit. Repeated business references must return the recorded outcome rather than initiate a new debit.

Example assumes authenticated authorization for the request and validated decimal input when creating it. It performs a local ledger withdrawal, not an external bank payment. The persisted request supplies both amount and account.

```php
DB::transaction(function () use ($requestId) {
    $request = WithdrawalRequest::whereKey($requestId)
        ->lockForUpdate()->firstOrFail();
    if ($request->status !== 'PENDING') return;

    $account = Account::whereKey($request->account_id)
        ->lockForUpdate()->firstOrFail();
    $amount = (string) $request->amount;
    if (!preg_match('/^[0-9]+(?:\.[0-9]{1,2})?$/D', $amount)
        || !preg_match('/[1-9]/', $amount)) {
        $request->update(['status' => 'FAILED', 'failure_code' => 'INVALID_AMOUNT']);
        return;
    }
    if ($account->status !== 'ACTIVE') {
        $request->update(['status' => 'FAILED', 'failure_code' => 'ACCOUNT_INACTIVE']);
        return;
    }
    // Bound SQL arithmetic preserves DECIMAL precision.
    $changed = DB::update(
        'UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?',
        [$amount, $account->id, $amount]
    );
    if ($changed !== 1) {
        $request->update(['status' => 'FAILED', 'failure_code' => 'INSUFFICIENT_FUNDS']);
        return;
    }
    AccountTransaction::create([
        'account_id' => $account->id, 'type' => 'WITHDRAWAL',
        'amount' => $amount, 'status' => 'SUCCESS',
        'reference_no' => 'WD-'.$request->id,
    ]);
    $request->update(['status' => 'SUCCESS']);
});
```

Approve a run only once under a row lock after checking the checker is authorized and different from the maker. Freeze totals and create durable outbox entries in the approval transaction. The outbox lets a dispatcher recover if the process crashes between commit and queue delivery.

For a simpler teaching example, Laravel supports afterCommit:

```php
DB::transaction(function () use ($runId) {
    $run = PayrollRun::whereKey($runId)->lockForUpdate()->firstOrFail();
    if ($run->status !== 'CALCULATED') return;
    // Authorization, maker/checker and frozen-total validation precede approval.
    $run->update(['status' => 'APPROVED']);
    $run->items()->orderBy('id')->chunkById(200, function ($items) {
        foreach ($items as $item) {
            $item->update(['status' => 'READY']);
            ProcessSalaryPayment::dispatch($item->id)->afterCommit();
        }
    });
});
// In the transaction that records confirmed payment success:
GenerateFeeReceipt::dispatch($payment->id)->afterCommit();
```

Workers must be idempotent. afterCommit prevents workers from seeing uncommitted data, but by itself is not a durable outbox. Never blindly resubmit UNKNOWN bank payments. Keep financial PDFs private and authorize every download.

HTTP/API -> Laravel -> MySQL -> Redis Queue -> Payment / Receipt / Notification / Reconciliation workers.

References: https://laravel.com/docs/10.x/queues#jobs-and-database-transactions and https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/innodb-locking-reads.html

